  "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y-x) \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}(2y)}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2-{e}^{y}) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
